INT. HOSPITAL - HYPERBARIC CHAMBER ANTEROOM - DAY

A small tiled room with a round porthole window into a steel pressure chamber. Gauges tick. A wall clock reads 2:15.

DR. SUNITA RAGHAVAN, 50s, scrubs and reading glasses, stands at the console reviewing a chart. She is relaxed. She is eating almonds.

NURSE FELIX OYELARAN, 30s, checks a valve with a gloved hand. Whistles faintly.

                    SUNITA
Pressure steady?

                    FELIX
Two point four. Just like the textbook.

                    SUNITA
Patient comfortable?

                    FELIX
Mr. Arkwright's asleep. He said the hum reminded him of his mother's refrigerator.

                    SUNITA
That's the nicest thing anyone's said about this machine.

They share a small laugh. The chamber hums.

DR. IMOGEN VASCO, 40s, hospital administrator's badge, steps in with a tablet and a face without expression.

                    IMOGEN
Dr. Raghavan. Nurse Oyelaran.

                    SUNITA
Imogen. Almond?

                    IMOGEN
No, thank you.

                    FELIX
Everything all right?

                    IMOGEN
I need a word about Mr. Arkwright.

                    SUNITA
Of course. What about him?

                    IMOGEN
His daughter called legal an hour ago.

                    SUNITA
His daughter. Gwen. She's lovely. She sat with him all morning.

                    IMOGEN
She says he was never told the full risk of the procedure.

                    SUNITA
I went through the consent form with her line by line.

                    IMOGEN
She says you did it while he was sedated.

                    FELIX
That's not what happened.

                    IMOGEN
I'm not saying it is.

                    SUNITA
Then what are you saying?

                    IMOGEN
I'm saying there's a record that says otherwise.

                    SUNITA
What record?

                    IMOGEN
The sedation log. It has a time on it. 1:40.

                    FELIX
That's wrong.

                    IMOGEN
It's what the system shows.

                    SUNITA
Felix, you charted the sedation.

                    FELIX
I did. At 2:00. After consent.

                    IMOGEN
The system says 1:40.

                    FELIX
Then someone changed it.

                    IMOGEN
Who could have?

                    FELIX
(slowly)
Dr. Raghavan.

                    SUNITA
What?

                    FELIX
You asked me for the keyboard at 1:50. To fix a typo.

                    SUNITA
I fixed a typo.

                    FELIX
You were on the sedation log.

                    SUNITA
Because I was checking the dose.

                    IMOGEN
Dr. Raghavan.

                    SUNITA
This is ridiculous.

                    IMOGEN
I have to ask you to step out of the chamber rotation while we review.

                    SUNITA
You can't be serious.

                    IMOGEN
It's procedure.

                    SUNITA
I have forty years of procedure. I know exactly what it is.

                    FELIX
I'm sorry. I didn't want to say it.

                    SUNITA
Say what, Felix?

                    FELIX
That I saw your hand move.

Sunita sets down the almonds. Takes off her glasses. Her voice is very quiet.

                    SUNITA
Pressure is two point four. In eleven minutes we need to bring him up. Who is going to do that, Imogen?

                    IMOGEN
Nurse Oyelaran is certified.

                    SUNITA
Then I suppose it will be Nurse Oyelaran.

                    FELIX
Dr. Raghavan, I--

                    SUNITA
Go on, Felix. The gauge won't watch itself.
